  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 209 AM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nThrough Saturday: A surface ridge which helped to limit rain \nchances yesterday will begin to lose influence in our region as it\nretreats eastward over the Atlantic. Across North MS and perhaps \ninto parts of AR and north LA, dewpoints will again mix down into \nthe 60s with limited rain chances again this afternoon. Further \nsouth, an increase in deep layer moisture will accompany a surface\ntrough lifting toward the Gulf Coast today. This will enhance \ndiurnal convection and result in greater coverage of \nafternoon/early evening convection over the south half of the \narea. By tomorrow, along with the influence of the northward \nmoving trough, a weak upper shortwave will approach the Midsouth \nwith a weak summertime front stalling to our north, resulting in \nhigher rain chances across a larger portion of the area than\ntoday. This regime will carry over into Saturday to some extent,\nthough the shortwave will weaken and shift eastward. \n\nIt will still be seasonably warm and humid over the next few days,\nand heat indices may approach or briefly reach advisory \nthresholds in a few spots, but the heat stress threat is expected \nto be tempered enough by mixing north and increased precip/clouds \nsouth to preclude the need for an advisory at this time. We will \nstill continue to highlight limited heat stress concerns through \nthe weekend. \n\nSunday through Wednesday: Upper ridging will begin to build back\nin across the Deep South early next week, leading us back to more\ntypical isolated/scattered summertime diurnal rain chances and \ntemperatures ticking back upward toward the upper 90s in some \nareas. With a rather muggy airmass still around, we may begin to \nsee heat indices again reaching critical thresholds and heat \nadvisories may ultimately be needed for some portion of the area. \n/DL/",
